{"results":[{"url":"https://www.yahoo.com/news/politics/articles/why-wisconsin-removed-nearly-500-100425962.html","final_url":"https://www.yahoo.com/news/politics/articles/why-wisconsin-removed-nearly-500-100425962.html","title":"Why Wisconsin removed nearly 500,000 from voter rolls before Nov. 3","description":"Wisconsin officials have removed nearly a half-million voters from the registration list since 2025 due to moves, deaths and felony convictions.","language":"en","text":"Return to Homepage\n\nWhy Wisconsin removed nearly 500,000 from voter rolls before Nov. 3\n\nLocal election officials have reviewed – and in many cases, deactivated – more than 500,000 voter registrations in Wisconsin over the last year as part of ongoing maintenance of the state's voter rolls.\n\nThe review doesn't target eligible voters whose registration is current, but instead makes sure that voters who have died, moved to another state, been convicted of a felony or been declared incompetent in court are removed from the state's active voter list and local poll books.\n\nAt an Oct. 19 news conference before the start of in-person absentee voting, Wisconsin Elections Commission Administrator Meagan Wolfe highlighted how clerks work daily to review the voter list and poll books.\n\nAdvertisement\n\nAdvertisement\n\nAdvertisement\n\nAdvertisement\n\n\"Making sure that the voter registration list is as accurate and up-to-date as possible is a task that our local election officials are undertaking every single day,\" Wolfe said.\n\n\"They get information from the Department of Vital Records, the Department of Corrections, about changes to voters' eligibility, or their address, and other information. All of those changes have resulted in Wisconsin's clerks updating or inactivating over 500,000 voter records to make sure our poll books are in the best possible condition before the Nov. 3 election.\"\n\nWisconsin's 1,850 city, town and village clerks are primarily responsible for making sure voter registration lists, poll books and absentee ballot logs are current. WEC staff also monitors 44 data checks and makes sure local clerks are acting on semi-automated alerts that flag voters to review.\n\nClerks also work to make sure a single person doesn't have multiple voter records. A voter logging into the state's MyVote system can find a list of elections they participated in, even if they've updated their address and registration over time.\n\nAdvertisement\n\nAdvertisement\n\n\"When we have voters that are moving from one location in Wisconsin to another, we pull them from that other jurisdiction into the current jurisdiction that they are registering, to ensure that there's only one voter record at all times,\" Karla Endres, president of the Wisconsin Municipal Clerks Association and clerk for the village of Waunakee, said on a recent call with reporters.\n\nOngoing maintenance ensures that \"lawful, registered voters appear on the poll book, and that only eligible voters are able to participate in the election,\" according to WEC's public election readiness report.\n\nThat report lists the following updates or deactivations this year or since 2025:\n\n* **187,036 inactive voters**: This number comes in part from WEC's review after each general election. WEC mails postcards to registered voters who have not voted within the past four years. Voters are removed if they don't respond.\n* **142,376 voters who moved:** That includes moves out-of-state, moves to a new municipality within the state and based on mail returned by the U.S. Postal Service.\n* **129,777 voters using ERIC**: Wisconsin is a member of the Electronic Registration Information Center, which helps states identify voters who moved, died or tried to cast ballots in two states, in part by using data from the Social Security Administration and USPS.\n* **31,708 voters who died:** Local clerks receive alerts based on monthly data from the Wisconsin Department of Health & Human Services' Vital Records Office and ERIC.\n* **2,575 based on a felony conviction:** Local clerks receive alerts based on nightly data from the Wisconsin Department of Corrections. People with felony convictions regain their right to vote once they've finished their sentence and are \"off paper.\"\n* **379 based on voters' requests**\n* More voters were deactivated because they were adjudicated incompetent in court. The WEC didn't provide an exact number, explaining that competency findings are confidential.\n\nAdvertisement\n\nAdvertisement\n\nWisconsin's voter rolls have been the subject of scrutiny from conservatives since the 2020 presidential election. False claims often circulate that Wisconsin has more registered voters than adults, which combines two separate lists –the active list, and an inactive list that voters move to if they die, move to a new state or are convicted of a felony.\n\n## How to check your Wisconsin voter registration\n\nTo check that you're registered to vote ahead of the Nov. 3 election, head to myvote.wi.gov. Enter your name and date of birth under \"My Voter Info\" to check that your registration is active and reflects your current address.\n\nIf you've moved or changed your name since the last election you voted in – possibly the Aug. 11 primary – you'll need to update your registration when you arrive at an early voting site or your polling place. In that case, bring a proof of residence document that shows your current address.\n\nAdvertisement\n\nAdvertisement\n\nAs of Oct. 1, Wisconsin had 3,617,544 registered voters. That number will rise when some voters register for the first time at an early voting site or at the polls on Election Day.\n\nWisconsin is anticipating more than 2.6 million eligible residents will vote in the Nov. 3 midterms, Wolfe said.\n\n*Hope Karnopp can be reached at HKarnopp@usatodayco.com.*\n\n*This article originally appeared on Milwaukee Journal Sentinel:* *Why Wisconsin removed nearly 500,000 from voter rolls before Nov. 3*\n\n* About Our Ads\n\nAdvertisement\n\nAdvertisement","author":"Hope Karnopp, Milwaukee Journal Sentinel","published_date":"2026-10-22T10:04:25Z","latency_ms":590.658094995888,"format":"markdown"}],"errors":[]}